INSERT INTO books (title, author, isbn, category, publisher, publication_year, total_copies, available_copies, description) VALUES
  ('Clean Architecture', 'Robert C. Martin', '9780134494166', 'Software Architecture', 'Prentice Hall', 2017, 3, 3, 'Clean Architecture is Robert Martin''s argument for building software whose core business rules stay separate from the tools used to deliver them. He starts from the idea that frameworks, databases, web servers and user interfaces are details, and that a well-structured system lets those details be swapped without disturbing the policies at its centre. The book revisits the SOLID design principles and then moves up a level to component design, covering how classes are grouped, which components should depend on which, and how to judge their stability and coupling. Its central device is the dependency rule, which says source code dependencies should point inward toward higher-level policy, usually pictured as concentric circles with entities and use cases at the middle. Later chapters discuss architectural boundaries, how to draw them, and what it costs to cross them, followed by short treatments of testing, databases, the web and frameworks as peripheral concerns. Martin also reflects on how an architecture should reveal the purpose of an application rather than its framework, and on why keeping options open is the real aim of good design. The tone is opinionated and anecdotal, drawing on decades of the author''s own experience. It suits working developers who want to move toward architectural responsibility, and experienced architects looking for a clear statement of one school of thought.'),
  ('Designing Data-Intensive Applications', 'Martin Kleppmann', '9781449373320', 'Software Architecture', 'O''Reilly Media', 2017, 4, 4, 'Martin Kleppmann''s book looks at the ideas underneath the databases, caches, queues and processing frameworks that modern applications rely on. Rather than surveying products one by one, it compares the trade-offs behind them so that readers can judge which tool fits a given problem. The opening part sets out reliability, scalability and maintainability as the goals, then examines data models and query languages, how storage engines lay data out on disk, and how data is encoded as it evolves over time. The second part turns to distributed data: replication, partitioning, transactions and isolation levels, the many ways distributed systems fail, and the meaning of consistency and consensus. The final part covers derived data, contrasting batch processing in the style of MapReduce with stream processing, and discusses how separate systems can be combined into one coherent application. Throughout, the author explains concepts carefully and points to research papers and industry sources for further study. The book assumes working knowledge of programming and some experience with databases, but it does not require specialist training. Software engineers, architects and technical leads who build or operate backend services are its main audience, as are readers preparing to design systems that must stay correct under load and partial failure.'),
  ('Fundamentals of Software Architecture', 'Mark Richards, Neal Ford', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'O''Reilly Media', 2020, 3, 3, 'This book offers a structured introduction to the work of a software architect, written for developers who are moving toward that role and for practising architects who want a common vocabulary. Mark Richards and Neal Ford begin by defining what architecture is, how it differs from design, and what an architect is expected to do beyond drawing diagrams. They then describe architecture characteristics, the qualities such as scalability, availability and testability that shape structural choices, and explain how to identify, measure and prioritise them. A large section surveys architecture styles, including layered, pipeline, microkernel, service-based, event-driven, space-based and microservices designs, and rates each against common characteristics so that strengths and weaknesses can be compared. The authors also address how to make and record architecture decisions, how to analyse and manage risk, and how to use fitness functions to keep a design from drifting. The closing chapters cover the non-technical side of the job, including negotiation, team leadership and communication. Trade-offs are the recurring theme, with the authors insisting that no style is best in every context. The writing is practical and example-driven. It is a good starting point for developers who want to understand architectural thinking before reading more specialised books on distributed systems or domain modelling.'),
  ('Software Architecture: The Hard Parts', 'Neal Ford, Mark Richards, Pramod Sadalage, Zhamak Dehghani', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'O''Reilly Media', 2021, 2, 2, 'This book picks up where introductory architecture texts leave off and concentrates on the difficult decisions that arise when systems are distributed. Its authors present trade-off analysis as the core skill, arguing that most real choices have no best answer, only a least bad one in a particular context. The material is framed around a fictional support-ticket company whose monolithic application must be broken up, and that running story ties the chapters together. The first sections cover how to decide whether and how to split a monolith, how to separate code and then data, and how granularity disintegrators and integrators pull the size of services in opposite directions. Later chapters deal with data ownership and shared data, the difficulty of keeping data consistent across services, and the contrast between orchestrated and choreographed workflows. The authors discuss distributed transactions and saga patterns, how to define contracts between services, and how analytical data fits alongside operational systems, including the data mesh approach. Each topic is presented as a set of options with their costs rather than as a recipe. Readers are expected to know architecture styles already. The book suits experienced developers, architects and technical leads who are designing or migrating systems built from many cooperating services.'),
  ('Building Microservices', 'Sam Newman', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'O''Reilly Media', 2021, 2, 2, 'Sam Newman''s book explains how to design, build and operate systems made of small, independently deployable services. It begins by defining what microservices are, what benefits they promise, and what problems they bring, with a balanced view that includes cases where a simpler architecture is the better choice. The early chapters cover modelling service boundaries, drawing on ideas from domain-driven design, and splitting an existing monolith step by step. The middle of the book is concerned with how services communicate, comparing synchronous and asynchronous styles, and with how workflows spanning several services can be coordinated. Practical material follows on build and deployment, testing strategies for distributed systems, and monitoring and observability once a system is running in production. Security, resilience and scaling each receive their own treatment, as do user interfaces and the shape of the organisation that owns the services. The author stresses that the approach brings real costs in complexity and that teams should adopt it only for clear reasons. Examples are technology-neutral, so the ideas apply across languages and platforms. Developers, architects and engineering managers who are considering microservices, or who already operate them and want to understand common failure modes, will find a broad overview that points onward to specialised literature.'),
  ('Domain-Driven Design', 'Eric Evans', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'Addison-Wesley', 2003, 2, 2, 'Eric Evans wrote this book for software teams working on projects whose difficulty comes from the business domain rather than the technology. His central claim is that the most valuable thing developers can do is build a rich model of the domain together with domain experts, and keep the code faithful to that model. The book introduces the ubiquitous language, a shared vocabulary used in conversation and in code, and the idea of model-driven design. A major section covers the tactical building blocks: entities, value objects, aggregates, repositories, factories, services and modules, along with advice on the life cycle of domain objects. Another section examines how models change through refactoring and deeper insight, including the pursuit of supple design. The final part addresses strategic design, where large systems are divided into bounded contexts, related through patterns such as context maps and anti-corruption layers, and where the core domain is separated from supporting parts. The text is dense, uses extended case studies, and rewards slow reading. It is widely cited as the origin of the domain-driven design movement. Developers, architects and analysts working on complex business systems will find it most useful, especially when they want a disciplined way to link requirements, language and code.'),
  ('Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture', 'Martin Fowler', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'Addison-Wesley', 2002, 1, 1, 'Martin Fowler''s book is a catalogue of patterns that recur in business applications, such as systems that manage orders, accounts or contracts and keep their data in relational databases. The first part is a set of narrative chapters on layering, on ways to organise domain logic, on mapping between objects and tables, on web presentation, and on concurrency and distribution. The larger second part is a reference, with each pattern described by its intent, how it works, when to use it, and a code example, written in Java and C#. Domain logic patterns such as transaction script, table module and domain model are compared, and the book explains how a service layer can sit above them. Data source patterns, including active record, data mapper and table data gateway, show different ways to connect code to a database. Further chapters cover object-relational behaviour such as unit of work and identity map, offline concurrency, session state, and web presentation patterns such as model view controller and front controller. Fowler is careful to present trade-offs, not prescriptions. Some technologies named in the examples have aged, but the underlying patterns still appear in modern frameworks. The book is aimed at experienced developers and architects who design layered enterprise systems and want a shared vocabulary for their choices.'),
  ('System Design Interview: An Insider''s Guide', 'Alex Xu', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'Independently published', 2020, 3, 3, 'Alex Xu''s guide prepares software engineers for the system design round of technical interviews, and it is also useful as a primer on designing large web services. It opens with a four-step framework for approaching an open-ended design question: understand the problem and set the scope, propose a high-level design, go deeper into selected components, and wrap up with a discussion of bottlenecks and improvements. The early chapters explain how to scale a system from a single server to millions of users, and how to make rough estimates of traffic, storage and capacity. Most of the book then works through individual case studies, including a rate limiter, consistent hashing, a key-value store, a unique ID generator, a URL shortener, a web crawler, a notification system, a news feed, a chat service, search autocomplete, a video streaming service and a file storage service. Each chapter uses many diagrams to show how components fit together and discusses the choices behind them. The explanations are brief and favour breadth over theory, so readers who want depth will need further sources. The book is aimed at candidates preparing for interviews, particularly those with limited experience of large distributed systems, and at developers who want an accessible introduction to common building blocks.'),
  ('Team Topologies', 'Matthew Skelton, Manuel Pais', NULL, 'Software Architecture', 'IT Revolution', 2019, 2, 2, 'Matthew Skelton and Manuel Pais argue that the way teams are organised has a direct effect on how quickly and safely software can be delivered. The book builds on Conway''s law, the observation that systems tend to mirror the communication structures of the organisations that build them, and suggests shaping teams on purpose so that the desired architecture can emerge. It limits team design to four fundamental types: stream-aligned teams that deliver a flow of work for a business area, enabling teams that help others adopt new capabilities, complicated-subsystem teams that own specialised components, and platform teams that provide internal services. It also describes three interaction modes between teams, which are collaboration, facilitating and consuming a service, and it encourages clear boundaries for each. Cognitive load is a recurring concept, with the authors proposing that a team''s responsibilities should fit within what its members can reasonably hold in their heads. The text covers how to evolve team structures over time as the organisation and its software change, and how to treat a platform as a product. Practical examples and diagrams support the ideas. Engineering managers, technical leaders, architects and agile coaches who need to design or reorganise software delivery teams will find it most relevant.'),

  ('Clean Code', 'Robert C. Martin', '9780132350884', 'Programming', 'Prentice Hall', 2008, 4, 4, 'Robert Martin''s handbook sets out his view of how professional programmers should write code that other people can read and maintain. It argues that the cost of messy code accumulates and that developers are responsible for keeping their work tidy as they go. The early chapters deal with the smallest elements: choosing meaningful names, writing short functions that do one thing, using comments sparingly, and formatting source files consistently. Later chapters discuss objects and data structures, error handling that does not obscure the main logic, drawing boundaries around third-party code, writing clean unit tests, and designing small, focused classes. Broader topics include the organisation of systems, the idea of emergent design through simple rules, and an introduction to concurrency. Several chapters step through extended cleanup exercises on real code, showing the reasoning behind each change. The final section is a catalogue of code smells and heuristics that can be used as a checklist during review. Examples are written mostly in Java, though the advice is meant to carry over to other object-oriented languages. Some of the guidance is opinionated and has been debated in the industry, so it is best read critically. It is aimed at working developers and students who want to improve the readability and long-term quality of their code.'),
  ('The Pragmatic Programmer', 'David Thomas, Andrew Hunt', '9780135957059', 'Programming', 'Addison-Wesley', 2019, 3, 3, 'David Thomas and Andrew Hunt present a collection of short, practical lessons on being an effective software developer. The material is organised as many brief topics, each ending in a numbered tip, and it covers attitude and habits as much as technique. Early sections encourage taking responsibility for one''s work, caring about craft, and continuing to learn through a deliberate knowledge portfolio. The book then turns to design and coding practice, including the principle of not repeating knowledge in multiple places, keeping components orthogonal so that changes stay local, and using tracer-bullet development to explore a problem early with working code. Other topics include debugging, making good use of plain text and command-line tools, writing code that is easy to change, handling concurrency, and testing ruthlessly. A later group of chapters addresses working with people and projects, such as estimating, communicating with users, and avoiding the slow decay of a codebase that is not looked after. The authors favour pragmatic judgement over rigid methodology, and they illustrate points with analogies and short anecdotes. This edition revises the original for current practice. It is suitable for developers at any career stage, and it is often given to those starting their first professional job.'),
  ('Refactoring', 'Martin Fowler', '9780134757599', 'Programming', 'Addison-Wesley', 2018, 2, 2, 'Martin Fowler''s book explains how to improve the internal design of existing code through a series of small steps that do not change its observable behaviour. It begins with a worked example in which a modest program is transformed step by step, illustrating the rhythm of making a change, running tests and moving on. The author then defines refactoring, explains why it matters for long-term productivity, and discusses when to do it and when not to. A chapter on code smells describes symptoms such as duplicated logic, long functions, large classes and feature envy that suggest a refactoring is due. The core of the book is a catalogue of individual refactorings, each documented with a name, a sketch, a motivation, a step-by-step mechanics section and an example. They range from extracting functions and renaming variables to moving behaviour between modules and replacing conditional logic with polymorphism. The book places strong emphasis on self-testing code, since tests are what make safe change possible. This edition presents its examples in JavaScript, and the ideas translate to most mainstream languages. It suits developers who maintain evolving codebases, team leads who want shared practices for improving legacy code, and students who want to move beyond writing code that only works.'),
  ('Design Patterns', 'Erich Gamma, Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson, John Vlissides', '9780201633610', 'Programming', 'Addison-Wesley', 1994, 2, 2, 'Design Patterns, often called the Gang of Four book after its four authors, introduced a catalogue of recurring solutions to common problems in object-oriented design. After an introduction explaining what a pattern is and how the catalogue is organised, the authors work through a case study in which a document editor is designed using several of the patterns. The core of the book describes twenty-three patterns arranged in three groups: creational patterns that deal with object creation, structural patterns that compose classes and objects into larger structures, and behavioural patterns that distribute responsibility and manage communication. Each entry follows a consistent template covering intent, motivation, applicability, structure, participants, collaborations, consequences, implementation notes and sample code, using C++ and Smalltalk. Two principles run through the text: program to an interface rather than an implementation, and favour object composition over class inheritance. The book is compact and written in a formal, reference-like style, which makes it demanding for beginners. Some patterns reflect the limits of the languages of its time, and later readers have debated how many remain necessary. Even so, it supplied a shared vocabulary that developers still use in design discussions, and it remains a standard reference for programmers and students of object-oriented design.'),
  ('Effective Java', 'Joshua Bloch', NULL, 'Programming', 'Addison-Wesley', 2018, 2, 2, 'Joshua Bloch''s book is a guide to writing clear, correct and maintainable Java, organised as a series of numbered items, each a short essay with advice and its rationale. The author draws on his experience designing parts of the Java platform libraries, so many items explain the reasoning behind choices that library users meet every day. Topics include creating and destroying objects, such as using static factory methods and builders, and the contracts that govern methods common to all objects, such as equals, hashCode and toString. The book then covers designing classes and interfaces, with guidance on favouring composition and keeping mutability under control, followed by generics, enumerations and annotations. Later chapters address lambdas and streams, the design of method signatures, general programming practice, exception handling, concurrency and serialization. This edition updates the material to cover features introduced in recent versions of the language. Each item names the problem, shows the pitfall and demonstrates a better alternative with code. Readers are expected to know the basics of Java, since the book does not teach syntax. It is aimed at intermediate and experienced Java developers who want to deepen their understanding of the language''s idioms, and at teams looking for a common reference during code review.'),
  ('Head First Design Patterns', 'Eric Freeman, Elisabeth Robson', NULL, 'Programming', 'O''Reilly Media', 2020, 3, 3, 'This book introduces object-oriented design patterns through a highly visual, conversational format that departs from the formal style of reference texts. Pages are filled with illustrations, puzzles, quizzes, informal dialogue and fictional conversations between patterns, all intended to help readers remember ideas through active engagement. Each chapter starts with a concrete design problem, often a small simulation or business scenario, shows a naive solution that becomes hard to change, and then derives a pattern that solves it. Patterns covered include strategy, observer, decorator, factory, singleton, command, adapter, facade, template method, iterator, composite, state and proxy, with a later chapter on compound patterns such as model view controller. Underlying design principles, such as encapsulating what varies, favouring composition over inheritance and programming to interfaces, are highlighted along the way. Code is written in Java, so some familiarity with the language and with basic object-oriented concepts is assumed. The authors keep explanations accessible, and exercises with answers let readers check their understanding. This updated edition adjusts the examples to reflect more recent language features. The book is most suitable for beginners and intermediate developers who find traditional pattern catalogues dry, and for self-taught programmers who want a gentle first look at design vocabulary.'),
  ('Test-Driven Development: By Example', 'Kent Beck', NULL, 'Programming', 'Addison-Wesley', 2002, 2, 2, 'Kent Beck''s short book teaches test-driven development by showing it in practice rather than by describing it abstractly. The central cycle is simple: write a failing test, make it pass as quickly as possible, then remove duplication and improve the design. The first of the book''s two extended examples builds a small multi-currency money model in Java, taking tiny steps and letting each new test guide the next piece of design. The second example develops a testing framework in the xUnit style, using the framework to test itself as it grows, and it illustrates how the approach scales from simple code to tools. A third part gathers patterns and techniques, including how to choose the next test, how to move from a quick fake implementation toward a general one, and when to take larger or smaller steps. The author also discusses refactoring, how to deal with fear and uncertainty while coding, and common questions about when the method works well. The writing is informal and conversational, with a notebook-like feel that records the author''s thinking as he works. Developers who are new to unit testing, teams considering adopting test-first practices, and programmers wanting to see the technique demonstrated in small increments will find it a clear starting point.'),
  ('Working Effectively with Legacy Code', 'Michael Feathers', NULL, 'Programming', 'Prentice Hall', 2004, 1, 1, 'Feathers starts from a blunt definition: legacy code is code that has no tests around it, regardless of its age or who wrote it. From that premise the book builds a practical method for changing such code without breaking it. The central idea is to find a seam, a place where behaviour can be altered without editing the code at that spot, so that a troublesome piece can be pulled into a test harness. The author describes characterization tests, which record what a system actually does today so that later edits can be checked against it. A large part of the book is a catalogue of dependency-breaking techniques, each presented with before-and-after code and a discussion of the risks involved. Other chapters tackle common predicaments, such as a class too large to understand, a method too long to test, or a deadline that leaves little time for cleanup. Examples are drawn mainly from Java, C++, C and C#, so the ideas carry across languages. The tone is pragmatic rather than idealistic, and the chapters are organised so that a developer can look up the specific problem in front of them. It suits engineers who maintain large, ageing systems, team leads weighing refactoring risk, and anyone who has inherited a codebase that nobody fully understands. Familiarity with object-oriented programming is assumed.'),
  ('Eloquent JavaScript', 'Marijn Haverbeke', NULL, 'Programming', 'No Starch Press', 2018, 3, 3, 'Haverbeke''s book is an introduction to programming that uses JavaScript as its language, and it is written for readers who may never have programmed before as well as for those who want a deeper grasp of the language. The first part covers the fundamentals: values, functions, control flow, data structures, higher-order functions, objects, and how to handle bugs and errors. The second part moves into the browser, explaining the document object model, events, and drawing on a canvas, along with HTTP and forms. The third part turns to Node.js and server-side work. Alongside the explanations, the author sets exercises at the end of each chapter and includes several larger projects, such as a small programming language, a platform game and a pixel-art editor. The writing is conversational but does not avoid abstraction, and it encourages readers to think about why code is shaped the way it is rather than only how to type it. The book is also published online with an interactive editor, so examples can be run and changed in place. It suits self-taught beginners, students, and developers coming from other languages who want a thoughtful tour of JavaScript''s ideas. Readers should expect to read slowly and work the exercises.'),
  ('Programming TypeScript', 'Boris Cherny', NULL, 'Programming', 'O''Reilly Media', 2019, 2, 2, 'Cherny''s book teaches TypeScript as a way to bring a strong, checkable type system to JavaScript applications that are growing in size and complexity. It begins with the compiler and the basic types, then builds up to functions, classes and interfaces, and explains how the type checker reasons about the code. Later chapters cover more demanding material, including generics, advanced type features such as mapped and conditional types, and the way types can describe the shape of real-world data. The author also addresses practical matters: handling errors, writing asynchronous code with promises and other concurrency tools, and structuring programs with modules and namespaces. A section on tooling explains how to build, run and configure a project, while another deals with using existing JavaScript libraries and gradually migrating an untyped codebase. Throughout, the emphasis is on understanding the reasoning behind the language''s design so that readers can make their own judgements rather than memorise rules. Examples are short and focused, and each chapter closes with exercises. The book is aimed at working developers who already know JavaScript and want to write safer, more maintainable code. It suits front-end and back-end programmers alike, and teams considering a move to TypeScript will find guidance on doing so step by step.'),
  ('Kotlin in Action', 'Dmitry Jemerov, Svetlana Isakova', NULL, 'Programming', 'Manning', 2017, 1, 1, 'Written by two members of the team at the company that created the language, this book introduces Kotlin to programmers who already know Java. It does not start from scratch; instead it shows how familiar Java ideas look in Kotlin and where the newer language departs from them. Early chapters cover basic syntax, functions, classes, and the way Kotlin handles nullability so that many common runtime errors are caught at compile time. Later sections explore lambdas and functional-style operations on collections, extension functions, and the type system, including generics and variance. A recurring theme is interoperability: Kotlin code can call Java libraries and be called from Java, and the authors explain how to mix the two in a single project and how to adopt Kotlin gradually. Further chapters introduce delegation, annotations and reflection, and the construction of domain-specific languages, which is one of the book''s more distinctive topics. The explanations lean on short examples and occasionally look beneath the surface at what the compiler generates. The audience is professional Java developers, including those working on Android or server applications, who want to understand not only how to write Kotlin but why its design choices were made. Prior Java experience is expected, and the pace is steady.'),

  ('Deep Learning', 'Ian Goodfellow, Yoshua Bengio, Aaron Courville', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'MIT Press', 2016, 2, 2, 'This textbook gives a broad and fairly rigorous account of deep learning, written by three prominent researchers in the field. It opens with the mathematical background needed to follow the rest: linear algebra, probability and information theory, and numerical computation, followed by a review of the basics of machine learning. The second part covers deep networks as used in practice, including feedforward networks, regularization, optimization methods, convolutional networks and recurrent and recursive networks, with guidance on practical methodology. The third part is more research-oriented and discusses topics such as linear factor models, autoencoders, representation learning, structured probabilistic models, Monte Carlo methods, approximate inference and deep generative models. The authors favour conceptual explanation and mathematical detail over code, so the book is not a programming tutorial and contains no step-by-step recipes for any particular framework. Chapters are written to be referenced as well as read straight through, and the text includes extensive citations to the research literature. It is intended for university students, researchers and software engineers who want a solid theoretical grounding and are comfortable with undergraduate-level mathematics. Readers seeking a gentler or more code-driven introduction may prefer to pair it with a practical guide, since the density rewards careful, patient study.'),
  ('Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow', 'Aurélien Géron', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'O''Reilly Media', 2022, 3, 3, 'Géron''s book is a practical introduction to machine learning that teaches through working Python code. It is organised in two main parts. The first covers the fundamentals using Scikit-Learn: an end-to-end project that walks from raw data to a trained model, then classification, regression, support vector machines, decision trees, ensemble methods and dimensionality reduction, along with unsupervised techniques such as clustering. The second part turns to neural networks and deep learning with Keras and TensorFlow, covering how to build, train and tune networks, work with convolutional networks for images, handle sequences, and apply attention-based models to language tasks. Later chapters also touch on generative models, reinforcement learning, and deploying and scaling models for production. The author explains the intuition behind each algorithm before showing the code, keeps the mathematics light, and supplies exercises and notebooks so readers can experiment. Advice on common pitfalls such as overfitting, poor data quality and hyperparameter selection appears throughout. The book assumes some Python programming experience but not a background in machine learning, and basic familiarity with calculus and linear algebra is helpful. It suits programmers, analysts and students who want to move from theory to building working systems and who learn best by running examples.'),
  ('Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach', 'Stuart Russell, Peter Norvig', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'Pearson', 2020, 2, 2, 'This is a comprehensive university textbook that surveys the whole field of artificial intelligence through the unifying idea of the intelligent agent, a system that perceives its environment and acts to achieve goals. The opening sections introduce agents and environments, then cover problem solving through search, including uninformed, heuristic and adversarial methods, as well as constraint satisfaction. Later parts examine knowledge and reasoning with logic, planning, and reasoning under uncertainty using probability and decision theory. Machine learning receives extensive treatment, spanning supervised learning, neural networks, deep learning, probabilistic models and reinforcement learning. Further chapters address natural language processing, computer vision and robotics. The closing sections consider the philosophical and practical questions raised by AI, including fairness, safety, and the long-term goal of building systems that remain beneficial to people. Each chapter ends with exercises and historical notes that place ideas in context, and the text balances mathematical precision with readable explanation. The authors write for undergraduate and graduate students, but the breadth also makes it a standard reference for researchers and engineers who want to see how the subfields connect. A background in basic programming, probability and discrete mathematics is useful, though the book is designed to be taught across one or two courses.'),
  ('Designing Machine Learning Systems', 'Chip Huyen', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'O''Reilly Media', 2022, 2, 2, 'Huyen focuses on a question that many tutorials skip: what does it take to get a machine learning model working reliably in a real product? The book treats machine learning as an iterative process rather than a single modelling exercise. It begins with project scoping and the differences between research and production settings, then moves through data engineering, training data creation, and feature engineering. Further chapters look at model development, offline evaluation and the trade-offs that influence the choice of approach. The deployment sections compare batch and online prediction and discuss how to serve models, while later chapters cover monitoring, the ways data distributions drift over time, and continual learning so that systems stay useful after launch. The author also considers infrastructure and tooling, the organisation of teams, and responsible practices such as fairness and privacy. Explanations favour principles and design reasoning over any one vendor''s tools, with case examples drawn from industry experience. The book assumes the reader already knows basic machine learning concepts and some programming. It suits data scientists who want to understand engineering concerns, software engineers moving into machine learning, and technical managers who need to plan and review production projects. Readers wanting algorithm derivations will need to look elsewhere.'),
  ('AI Engineering', 'Chip Huyen', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'O''Reilly Media', 2025, 3, 3, 'This book addresses the practice of building applications on top of foundation models rather than training new models from scratch. Huyen opens with an account of what has changed as large pre-trained models became widely available, and what the new role of an AI engineer involves. The text then explains how foundation models work at a level useful for application builders, including sampling and the sources of unreliable output. A major portion is devoted to evaluation, arguing that measuring open-ended model behaviour is one of the hardest problems in the field and surveying methods for doing it. Other chapters cover prompt engineering and defences against misuse, retrieval-augmented generation, agents and tool use, fine-tuning, dataset engineering, and techniques for making inference faster and cheaper. The closing material discusses how to combine these pieces into a complete architecture and how to gather user feedback to improve a product over time. The author concentrates on durable concepts and trade-offs instead of tying the advice to one provider or library. The book is written for software engineers, machine learning practitioners and technical product leads who are building or planning AI-powered products. A working knowledge of programming and basic machine learning ideas will make the material easier to follow.'),
  ('Python for Data Analysis', 'Wes McKinney', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'O''Reilly Media', 2022, 2, 2, 'Written by the original author of the pandas library, this book is a practical guide to manipulating, processing and cleaning data with Python. It starts with the essential tools: the IPython and Jupyter environments, Python language basics, and the NumPy library for array computing. The core of the book then turns to pandas, showing how to load data from files and databases, clean and prepare it, handle missing values, and combine, reshape and aggregate datasets. Separate chapters cover plotting and visualization, group operations and pivot tables, and the handling of time series data. A closing section introduces modelling libraries such as statsmodels and scikit-learn, giving a sense of how data preparation connects to analysis and prediction. Concrete case studies show the techniques applied to realistic datasets, and code examples are designed to be typed or run along with the text. The author explains not only what each function does but also the conventions and idioms that experienced users rely on to write concise and efficient code. The book assumes readers can read basic Python, though it does not require prior statistics or data science experience. It suits analysts, scientists, students and engineers who work with tabular, numerical or time-stamped data and want a dependable reference they can return to.'),
  ('Storytelling with Data', 'Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'Wiley', 2015, 2, 2, 'Knaflic addresses a common workplace problem: people have data and charts but struggle to communicate what the numbers mean. Drawing on her experience teaching presentation workshops, she frames the task as one of storytelling, in which the visual is a means of getting an audience to understand and act. The book is organised around a series of lessons. These begin with understanding the context, meaning who the audience is, what they need to know and how best to convey it. Next comes choosing an appropriate visual display, with attention to why some common chart types work well and others mislead. The author then shows how to remove clutter, direct attention to the most important elements using colour, size and position, and think like a designer when arranging a page. Later chapters draw the threads together into a narrative structure, supported by before-and-after makeovers of real-looking examples and several longer case studies. The text relies on ideas from visual perception, such as how viewers notice differences before reading details. It is aimed at business professionals, analysts, managers and students who prepare reports or presentations, and it requires no design background or specialist software. The approach is tool-agnostic, so the principles apply whether charts are made in spreadsheets or dedicated visualization packages.'),
  ('Weapons of Math Destruction', 'Cathy O''Neil', NULL, 'Data & AI', 'Crown', 2016, 1, 1, 'O''Neil, a mathematician who worked in finance and later in data science, examines how mathematical models are used to make decisions about people, and what happens when those models go wrong. She introduces a category she calls weapons of math destruction, which are models marked by three features: they are opaque to the people they affect, they operate at large scale, and they cause real damage. The book moves through a series of domains to illustrate the pattern, including teacher evaluation, university rankings, policing and sentencing, credit and insurance, hiring practices, and online advertising. In each case she argues that models trained on historical data can reproduce existing bias, that proxies stand in for things that are hard to measure, and that feedback loops can make a flawed prediction come true. Errors tend to fall hardest on those with the least power to contest them, which is why she links the issue to inequality and democratic accountability. The author also offers some thoughts on remedies, such as auditing algorithms, demanding transparency, and building ethical considerations into the design. The book is written for general readers rather than specialists, with no mathematics required. It will interest students, policymakers, journalists, data professionals and anyone curious about the social effects of automated decisions.'),

  ('To Kill a Mockingbird', 'Harper Lee', '9780061120084', 'Fiction', 'Harper Perennial', 1960, 3, 3, 'Set in the fictional town of Maycomb, Alabama, during the Great Depression, this novel is narrated by Scout Finch, a young girl looking back on a formative stretch of her childhood. She lives with her older brother Jem and their widowed father, Atticus, a respected lawyer who tries to raise his children with fairness and patience. During the long summers the children and their friend Dill are fascinated by their reclusive neighbour Boo Radley, whom the town regards with suspicion and rumour. Meanwhile, the community is shaken when Atticus accepts the task of defending a Black man accused of a serious crime against a white woman. As the case draws closer, Scout and Jem confront the prejudice and social divisions of the town, and they begin to see how people they know behave when pressured by fear and custom. The book explores racial injustice, moral courage, empathy, class and the loss of childhood innocence. Its structure combines episodes of ordinary small-town life with a central courtroom drama, and its humour sits beside serious themes. It is widely read in schools and appeals to general readers, particularly those interested in American literature and the history of civil rights. Its language is accessible, and its narrator''s point of view gives familiar events a fresh perspective.'),
  ('1984', 'George Orwell', '9780451524935', 'Fiction', 'Signet Classics', 1949, 4, 4, 'Orwell''s novel imagines a future in which much of the world is divided among three rival superstates, and Britain has become a province called Airstrip One within Oceania. The ruling Party, personified by the ever-watching figure of Big Brother, governs through constant surveillance, with telescreens in homes and a Thought Police that punishes disloyalty even in private thinking. The central character is Winston Smith, a low-ranking worker at the Ministry of Truth, where his job is to alter historical records so that they match whatever the Party currently claims. Increasingly uneasy about the lies he helps produce, Winston begins a secret diary and starts to look for ways to assert his own sense of reality and personal feeling. The story follows his private rebellion and the risks it carries in a society where trust has been destroyed. Its themes include the manipulation of truth, the control of language through the invented tongue Newspeak, state power, and the fragility of individual freedom. The book is short on comfort and tightly focused on its protagonist''s inner life. Terms from it, such as doublethink and Orwellian, have passed into everyday speech. It suits general readers, students, and anyone interested in political fiction, and it is frequently discussed in connection with debates about privacy and propaganda.'),
  ('The Hobbit', 'J.R.R. Tolkien', '9780547928227', 'Fiction', 'Houghton Mifflin Harcourt', 1937, 3, 3, 'Tolkien''s fantasy begins in the quiet Shire, where Bilbo Baggins, a comfortable and rather conventional hobbit, is visited by the wizard Gandalf and a company of thirteen dwarves led by Thorin Oakenshield. They persuade Bilbo to join an expedition to recover treasure held by the dragon Smaug in the Lonely Mountain, with Bilbo cast as the party''s burglar. The journey takes them across Middle-earth through wild country, where they meet trolls, goblins, giant spiders, elves and other creatures. Along the way Bilbo discovers resources of cleverness and courage he did not know he had, including a memorable contest of riddles beneath the mountains. The story follows a classic quest pattern but gives it a warm, wry narrative voice that addresses the reader directly and treats danger with a touch of humour. Its themes include the pull between comfort and adventure, greed and its effects on those who seek treasure, loyalty among companions, and the way ordinary people can rise to extraordinary events. Written originally for children, it is enjoyed equally by adults, and it serves as an entry point to the author''s larger invented world. The prose is straightforward, the pacing brisk, and the maps and songs add to the sense of a fully imagined place.'),
  ('Pride and Prejudice', 'Jane Austen', NULL, 'Fiction', 'Penguin Classics', 1813, 2, 2, 'Austen''s novel is set among the landed gentry of rural England in the early nineteenth century, where marriage is bound up with money, property and social standing. The story centres on the Bennet family, whose five daughters stand to lose their home because the estate can pass only to a male relative. Mrs Bennet is determined to see her daughters well married, and her hopes rise when the wealthy and amiable Mr Bingley rents a nearby house. His friend, the proud and reserved Mr Darcy, makes a poor first impression on the spirited and quick-witted second daughter, Elizabeth. As the two characters meet repeatedly, they must reconsider their assumptions about each other, and about their own failings. The plot also involves the charm of an officer named Wickham, the family''s embarrassments, and the pressures of class distinction. The novel is known for its ironic narrative voice, its sharp dialogue and its precise portrayal of social behaviour. Central themes include the unreliability of first impressions, the role of pride, the limited options available to women, and the possibility of marrying for affection rather than convenience. It is a standard text in school and university courses and remains a popular choice for readers of classic fiction and romantic comedy, with a witty style that rewards slow reading.'),
  ('The Great Gatsby', 'F. Scott Fitzgerald', NULL, 'Fiction', 'Scribner', 1925, 2, 2, 'This short novel is set on Long Island and in New York City during one summer in the early 1920s. Its narrator, Nick Carraway, is a young Midwesterner who moves east to work in the bond business and rents a small house beside the mansion of Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire known for his enormous parties. Nick is drawn into Gatsby''s circle and slowly learns what lies behind the spectacle: a long-held longing for Daisy Buchanan, Nick''s cousin, who is married to the wealthy and domineering Tom. Through these characters Fitzgerald examines the American Dream, the divide between inherited and newly made fortunes, and the distance between the self a person presents and the life he actually leads. The book also serves as a portrait of the Jazz Age, with its prosperity, restlessness and loosening morals. Written in compact, lyrical prose and carefully structured, it can be read in a single sitting. It is widely taught in schools and universities, and it suits readers meeting American literature for the first time as well as those returning to it. Memory, class, desire and disillusion are its central concerns. Libraries commonly shelve it among the twentieth-century classics of English-language fiction.'),
  ('One Hundred Years of Solitude', 'Gabriel García Márquez', NULL, 'Fiction', 'Harper Perennial', 1967, 2, 2, 'This novel follows seven generations of the Buendia family in Macondo, a remote town in Colombia founded by José Arcadio Buendia and his wife Úrsula. What begins as an isolated settlement gradually meets the outside world through visiting traveller-inventors, civil wars, railways and a foreign fruit company. García Márquez tells the story in the manner now called magical realism, treating extraordinary events such as prophecies, ghosts and plagues of insomnia with the same calm tone as everyday matters. Family members repeat names and temperaments across the generations, so the reader watches obsessions, loves and mistakes come round again. Solitude, fate, memory and the cyclical nature of time are the central themes, along with a reflection on Latin American political history. The narrative moves freely in time and is crowded with characters, so many readers keep the family tree at the front of the book close at hand. The English translation is by Gregory Rabassa. It is often recommended to readers interested in world literature, Latin American writing, or fiction that blends the fantastic with social observation. Patient readers will find a richly imagined, humorous and sorrowful saga.'),
  ('The Alchemist', 'Paulo Coelho', NULL, 'Fiction', 'HarperOne', 1988, 3, 3, 'This fable-like novel follows Santiago, a young Andalusian shepherd who has a recurring dream about treasure hidden near the Egyptian pyramids. Encouraged by an old king he meets, he sells his flock and crosses into North Africa to pursue it. Along the way he is robbed, works for a crystal merchant, joins a caravan across the desert and falls in love at an oasis. He also meets an Englishman devoted to the study of alchemy, and eventually the alchemist of the title, who becomes his guide. The story is built around the idea of a Personal Legend, the notion that each person has a destiny worth following, and around learning to read omens and listen to the heart. Fear, comfort and the pull of ordinary security appear as obstacles to that pursuit. Coelho writes in short, plain, parable-like prose, and the book is quick to read. It was originally written in Portuguese and has been translated into very many languages. Readers who enjoy spiritual allegory, quest stories and uplifting reflective fiction are its main audience, though critics have found its message simple.'),
  ('Palace Walk', 'Naguib Mahfouz', NULL, 'Fiction', 'Anchor', 1956, 2, 2, 'This is the first volume of the Cairo Trilogy, a family saga set in Cairo between 1917 and 1919, during the First World War and the nationalist uprising against British rule. The household of Ahmad Abd al-Jawad, a shopkeeper in the old district around Bayn al-Qasrayn, is the centre of the story. At home he is a strict, authoritarian father who demands obedience from his submissive wife Amina and their children, while outside he enjoys a lively social life of friends, music and pleasure. The children, Yasin, Fahmy, Khadija, Aisha and Kamal, each begin to test the limits of his rule in their own way. Fahmy is drawn toward the political movement sweeping the city, which brings the family into contact with public events. Mahfouz uses this one household to explore tradition and change, religion, gender roles, class and the pull between generations in Egyptian society. The novel is realist in style, detailed in its portrayal of streets, homes and customs, and it is available in English translation. It suits readers interested in Arabic literature, Egyptian history, or long family sagas, and it leads into the trilogy''s later volumes.'),
  ('The Name of the Wind', 'Patrick Rothfuss', NULL, 'Fiction', 'DAW Books', 2007, 2, 2, 'This epic fantasy is the first book of the Kingkiller Chronicle. It opens in a quiet inn, where the innkeeper Kote is revealed to be Kvothe, a figure about whom legends and rumours circulate throughout the land. A travelling scribe called the Chronicler persuades him to tell the true story of his life, and the narrative becomes Kvothe''s own account, recounted over a single day. He recalls growing up in a troupe of travelling performers, a tragedy that leaves him alone and struggling on city streets, and his eventual admission to a university where students study magic, medicine and the lore of names. There he faces poverty, a rival named Ambrose, demanding masters and a complicated attachment to a young woman called Denna. The magic is presented as a system with rules, including a practice called sympathy, and music is central to the hero''s talent. The story is told in a leisurely, lyrical style that dwells on character and detail rather than constant battle. It suits readers who enjoy long, immersive fantasy with a gifted and sometimes unreliable narrator, and it works as the start of a larger series.'),
  ('Project Hail Mary', 'Andy Weir', NULL, 'Fiction', 'Ballantine Books', 2021, 2, 2, 'This science fiction novel begins with a man waking aboard a spacecraft with no memory of who he is or why he is there. As his recollections return, he recognises himself as Ryland Grace, a former molecular biologist turned middle-school science teacher, and he gradually learns that he is the sole surviving crew member of a desperate mission. The Sun is being dimmed by a microscopic organism, and the voyage to a distant star is humanity''s attempt to find out why and whether the process can be stopped. Narrated in the first person with a dry sense of humour, the book alternates between present-day problem solving on the ship and flashbacks that fill in how the mission came about. Midway through, Grace encounters an alien visitor, and their efforts to communicate and cooperate form the emotional core of the story. Weir draws on physics, chemistry and engineering, explaining each step clearly. Friendship, sacrifice and scientific curiosity are central themes. The novel suits readers who like hard science fiction with an optimistic tone, and it will appeal to those who enjoyed the author''s earlier work about survival on Mars.'),

  ('A Brief History of Time', 'Stephen Hawking', NULL, 'Science', 'Bantam', 1988, 2, 2, 'Written for readers without a scientific background, this book introduces the main ideas of modern cosmology and theoretical physics. Hawking begins with how people have pictured the universe over the centuries, from ancient models to Newton and then to Einstein''s theories of relativity. He explains the expanding universe, the idea that the cosmos had a beginning, and what the concept of space-time means. Later chapters discuss black holes, how they form and why they might emit radiation, as well as the uncertainty principle from quantum mechanics and the elementary particles and forces of nature. A recurring theme is the search for a single, unified theory that would reconcile general relativity with quantum mechanics. He also considers the arrow of time, asking why we remember the past but not the future. The author keeps mathematics to a minimum and relies on analogies and diagrams, though some passages still demand slow, careful reading. The book sets out the questions physicists were asking about the origin and fate of the universe, and it gives non-specialists a clear route into the subject. It is commonly recommended as an entry point for curious general readers and students.'),
  ('The Selfish Gene', 'Richard Dawkins', NULL, 'Science', 'Oxford University Press', 1976, 2, 2, 'Dawkins presents a way of understanding evolution that puts the gene, rather than the organism or the species, at the centre. In his account, genes are replicators that copy themselves down the generations, and living bodies are machines that genes build to help them survive and spread. This gene''s-eye view is used to explain behaviour that might otherwise seem puzzling, including cooperation among relatives, parental care, conflict between mates and apparently selfless acts. Along the way he introduces ideas from game theory, such as evolutionarily stable strategies, to show how competing behaviours can persist in a population. He argues that natural selection acts at the level of genes, while cautioning that genes do not make individuals consciously selfish. Near the end he proposes the meme, a unit of cultural transmission that copies itself between minds, a concept that later became widely used outside biology. The writing is lively and aimed at non-specialists, with later editions adding notes that respond to critics and clarify points. It is often read by students of biology and by general readers who want an introduction to modern evolutionary thinking, and it remains a frequent subject of discussion.'),
  ('Cosmos', 'Carl Sagan', NULL, 'Science', 'Random House', 1980, 2, 2, 'Cosmos was written alongside a television series and presents the history of astronomy and science to a general audience. Sagan moves from the scale of the universe, from planets and stars to galaxies, to the story of how people came to understand it. He profiles scientists such as Eratosthenes and Kepler and describes the great library of Alexandria as a symbol of ancient curiosity. The chapters also cover the evolution of life on Earth, the life cycles of stars, the origin of the chemical elements and the possibility of life elsewhere. The book uses the idea of a cosmic calendar to compress the whole history of the universe into a single year, which helps readers grasp the vast timescales involved. Throughout, Sagan stresses the scientific method, healthy scepticism and the dangers of pseudoscience and nuclear war. His prose is poetic and personal, and he emphasises how small and fragile the Earth appears against the cosmos. It suits readers who want a broad, humane introduction to astronomy and the habits of scientific thinking, as well as those curious about how earlier generations tried to make sense of the sky.'),
  ('The Gene: An Intimate History', 'Siddhartha Mukherjee', NULL, 'Science', 'Scribner', 2016, 1, 1, 'Mukherjee, a physician and cancer researcher, traces the history of the gene from early ideas about heredity to the technologies of the present. He begins with Gregor Mendel and his experiments with pea plants, then follows Darwin, the rise of genetics as a discipline and the discovery of the structure of DNA. The book also treats the darker episodes of the subject, notably the eugenics movements of the twentieth century and their consequences. Later chapters describe the effort to read the human genome, the search for genes linked to disease, and the ethical questions raised by genetic testing and gene therapy. Interwoven with this scientific narrative is a personal thread, as the author reflects on mental illness that has affected members of his own family and what inheritance might mean for them. The writing combines biography, laboratory detail and memoir, and it explains technical ideas in everyday language. It is aimed at general readers and students who want a narrative overview of genetics and its social meaning. The book asks how much of who we are is determined by our genes and how far society should go in altering them.'),
  ('Astrophysics for People in a Hurry', 'Neil deGrasse Tyson', NULL, 'Science', 'W. W. Norton', 2017, 3, 3, 'This compact book gives readers a quick overview of the universe, written for those who want the essentials without technical detail. Tyson organises it as a series of short chapters, each focused on a single topic that can be read in a few minutes. It begins with the origin of the cosmos and the Big Bang, then moves through the formation of galaxies, stars and the chemical elements that make up planets and people. Later chapters introduce dark matter and dark energy, the invisible components that appear to dominate the cosmos, and describe how astronomers study the universe through the different kinds of light. He also explains that the same physical laws appear to apply everywhere, which lets scientists draw conclusions about distant objects. The tone is conversational and often humorous, with comparisons drawn from daily life. Because of its brevity, the book does not attempt depth, and readers wanting full explanations will need further reading. It is best suited to beginners, busy readers and students looking for an accessible first introduction, and it can serve as a refresher on the main ideas of astronomy and cosmology.'),
  ('The Body: A Guide for Occupants', 'Bill Bryson', NULL, 'Science', 'Doubleday', 2019, 2, 2, 'Bryson turns his attention from travel and history to the human body, which he treats as a remarkable, intricate and still partly mysterious machine. The book proceeds through its organs and systems, including the skin, the brain, the heart and circulation, the immune system, digestion, the senses and the microbes that live on and in us. Each section combines present-day scientific findings with stories of the researchers and physicians who uncovered them, including some who made errors or went unrecognised. He draws attention to how much remains unknown, even about common conditions, and he describes both the fragility of the body and its resilience. Humour is a constant feature, as is a sense of wonder at how many processes run without our noticing. The writing is clear and anecdotal and requires no medical background. It is aimed at general readers who want to understand their own bodies better, and it can also interest students and those who enjoy popular science with a human touch. The book does not offer health advice or diagnosis; it is intended to inform and entertain by explaining how the body works.'),

  ('Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind', 'Yuval Noah Harari', '9780062316097', 'History', 'Harper', 2015, 4, 4, 'Harari surveys the whole span of human history, from the emergence of Homo sapiens in Africa to the present day, and asks how one species came to dominate the planet. He organises the narrative around three great shifts: the cognitive revolution, when language and imagination allowed large groups to cooperate around shared stories; the agricultural revolution, which he treats as a mixed blessing for humans and for the animals they domesticated; and the scientific revolution, which gave people new power over nature. A central argument is that large-scale societies depend on collective fictions such as money, nations, religions and legal systems, which work because enough people believe in them. The book also discusses the unification of humankind through trade, empires and faiths, and it asks whether greater power has made people happier. The final chapters speculate about the future, including biotechnology and artificial intelligence. Written in a lively, opinionated style for a general readership, it covers a great deal of ground quickly, so specialists may find parts of it contested or simplified. It suits readers who want a broad, thought-provoking overview of the human story rather than a detailed chronicle.'),
  ('Guns, Germs, and Steel', 'Jared Diamond', NULL, 'History', 'W. W. Norton', 1997, 2, 2, 'Diamond sets out to answer a question posed to him by a New Guinean friend: why did some peoples develop advanced technology and powerful states while others did not? He rejects explanations based on any inherent differences between peoples and looks instead to geography and environment. Societies that had access to many plants and animals suitable for domestication were able to produce food surpluses, support dense populations and develop specialised occupations, writing and organised governments. The orientation of the continents also mattered, since innovations spread more readily across Eurasia than across the Americas or Africa. Livestock brought with it infectious diseases to which their owners acquired some immunity, and these diseases later devastated populations that had never encountered them. The book uses case studies, including the meeting of the Spanish and the Inca at Cajamarca, to show these factors at work. It covers a very long span of prehistory and history in a readable, argumentative style aimed at general readers. Some historians have criticised it for giving too little weight to human choices and institutions. It remains a common starting point for discussions about why global inequalities arose.'),
  ('The Silk Roads', 'Peter Frankopan', NULL, 'History', 'Bloomsbury', 2015, 2, 2, 'Frankopan argues that world history is usually told from a western European viewpoint, and he proposes instead to put the lands between East and West at the centre. His focus is the network of trade routes and connections that ran across Central Asia, Persia and the Middle East, linking Europe with China and India. The narrative begins in antiquity with early empires and follows the movement of goods, religions, ideas and armies along these routes. It considers the spread of Christianity and Islam, the Mongol conquests and the later rivalry of European powers for access to the East. In its later chapters it turns to the twentieth century, when oil, great-power competition and the two world wars brought attention back to the same regions. The book offers a sweeping survey that covers many peoples and centuries, so each episode is treated briefly. The author writes in an energetic, narrative style for general readers. It suits those who want a wider geographical perspective on history, are interested in the Middle East and Central Asia, or wish to see how trade shaped politics, religion and culture across the continents.'),
  ('A History of the Arab Peoples', 'Albert Hourani', NULL, 'History', 'Faber & Faber', 1991, 1, 1, 'Hourani offers a single-volume history of the Arab world from the rise of Islam in the seventh century through to the late twentieth century. Rather than concentrating only on rulers and wars, he describes how societies were organised, how cities and rural communities lived, and how religion, law and learning shaped everyday life. The account begins with Arabia and the Prophet Muhammad, follows the early caliphates and the formation of an Arab-Muslim empire, and then traces the later political fragmentation and the long period of Ottoman rule. In the later chapters he covers the impact of European power, economic change, the growth of nationalist movements and the new states that emerged in the modern period. The book pays attention to culture, language and thought as well as to politics, and it draws on a lifetime of scholarship by a leading historian of the Middle East. The prose is clear and measured, written for students and general readers rather than specialists. It is often used as an introductory text on Middle Eastern history and as a reference point for those seeking context on the region''s long-term development.'),
  ('The Rise and Fall of Ancient Egypt', 'Toby Wilkinson', NULL, 'History', 'Random House', 2010, 2, 2, 'Wilkinson, an Egyptologist, tells the story of pharaonic Egypt from the first settlements along the Nile to the arrival of Rome. The book follows the unification of the country under a single ruler and the long sequence of dynasties that followed. It pays close attention to the Nile itself, arguing that the river''s predictable rhythms and the narrow strip of fertile land shaped how the state was organised and how its kings thought about power. Kingship is the central thread: the pharaoh as a divine figure, the ideology that supported him, and the court, priesthood and army that carried out his will. The narrative moves through the great building age of the pyramids, the periods when central rule fractured, the revival of the Middle Kingdom, and the empire of the New Kingdom with its military campaigns abroad. Later chapters describe foreign domination and the slow weakening of the old order. Wilkinson combines archaeological evidence with royal inscriptions and written records, and he explains how historians read them. The tone is narrative rather than technical, with portraits of individual rulers set against wider political change. It suits general readers who want a single readable account of Egypt''s long history, as well as students seeking an overview before turning to more specialised studies of particular periods.'),
  ('SPQR: A History of Ancient Rome', 'Mary Beard', NULL, 'History', 'Liveright', 2015, 1, 1, 'Beard offers a history of Rome from its earliest traces to the point when citizenship was extended to nearly all free inhabitants of the empire. Rather than a simple list of emperors and battles, the book asks how a small settlement in central Italy came to rule so much of the Mediterranean world, and what that rule meant for the people inside and outside the city. It examines the legends of Romulus and the early kings and explains how little can be known with certainty about them. It then follows the Republic, its rivalries among powerful families, the struggles between rich and poor, and the civil wars that ended it. The rise of Augustus and the imperial system follow, with attention to everyday life, slavery, religion, law and the experience of ordinary Romans, not only of elites. Beard repeatedly stops to ask what the sources actually tell us and who wrote them, which gives the narrative a questioning, critical edge. The prose is lively and conversational, and the argument encourages readers to reconsider familiar stories such as the conspiracy against the Republic told by Cicero. The book is aimed at general readers and students who want an up-to-date and thoughtful introduction to Roman history.'),

  ('The Lean Startup', 'Eric Ries', NULL, 'Business', 'Crown Business', 2011, 3, 3, 'Ries presents a method for building new businesses and products under conditions of extreme uncertainty. Drawing on lean manufacturing ideas and on his own experience in startups, he argues that the main task of a young company is to discover as quickly as possible what customers really want, rather than to execute a detailed plan. The central idea is the build-measure-learn loop: turn an assumption into a small experiment, release it, collect data, and decide what the results mean. The book introduces the minimum viable product, an early version made with the least effort needed to test a hypothesis, and explains validated learning as the real measure of progress. It distinguishes metrics that show genuine traction from vanity figures that merely look encouraging, and describes innovation accounting as a way of holding teams accountable without fixed forecasts. A recurring decision is whether to pivot, changing strategy while keeping the vision, or to persevere. Later chapters cover small batches, growth engines and building organisations that can keep experimenting as they scale. Examples come from software firms and from larger companies. The book is written for founders, product teams and managers inside established firms who want a disciplined, evidence-based way to launch new ideas and reduce wasted effort.'),
  ('Good to Great', 'Jim Collins', NULL, 'Business', 'HarperBusiness', 2001, 2, 2, 'Collins and his research team set out to learn why a small number of companies moved from ordinary results to sustained, outstanding performance, while comparable firms did not. They studied financial records over several decades and compared each successful company with a similar firm that stayed average. The book presents the patterns they found as a set of connected concepts. Among them is Level 5 leadership, which pairs personal humility with fierce professional resolve. Another is the idea of getting the right people on the bus before deciding where it should go. Others include confronting the brutal facts of a situation while keeping faith in eventual success, and the hedgehog concept, a simple focus built on what a company can be best at, what drives its economics and what its people care about. Collins also describes a culture of discipline and the flywheel, in which steady, cumulative effort builds momentum instead of a single dramatic breakthrough. The argument is that greatness comes from consistent choices rather than charismatic reinvention. Readers should note that it is a business study based on a selected sample and that later commentary has questioned how lasting some of the featured companies proved to be. It is aimed at executives, managers and anyone interested in organisational performance.'),
  ('The Manager''s Path', 'Camille Fournier', NULL, 'Business', 'O''Reilly Media', 2017, 3, 3, 'Fournier writes a practical guide to engineering leadership organised around the stages of a technical career in management. It begins with the earliest step, mentoring a colleague, and moves through becoming a tech lead, managing individual engineers, overseeing several teams, leading a department and finally working at the level of senior executive. Each stage is treated as a separate job with its own skills, risks and expectations, so a reader can see what changes when responsibility grows. Topics include running regular one-to-one meetings, giving feedback, handling performance problems, hiring and growing a team, managing projects and dealing with conflict. In the middle chapters the focus shifts to managing managers, building culture and balancing technical judgement with business priorities. Throughout, the author draws on her own experience in engineering organisations of different sizes, and the advice is concrete and conversational rather than theoretical. The book also addresses the question of whether to move into management at all, and how to return to individual work if it is not the right fit. It is meant for engineers who are considering a leadership role, new managers who want a reference, and experienced leaders who want to coach those who report to them. Readers do not need a deep technical background to follow most of it.'),
  ('An Elegant Puzzle', 'Will Larson', NULL, 'Business', 'Stripe Press', 2019, 2, 2, 'Larson collects ideas about engineering management into a single framework built around the notion that running an engineering organisation is a problem of systems. Instead of offering a fixed list of rules, the book shows how to think about recurring questions and apply tools to each. It looks at how to decide the size of teams, how to grow them and how to arrange reporting lines so that work flows without constant coordination. It discusses how to plan and set priorities, how to measure progress without distorting behaviour, and how to bring new engineers up to speed. A large part of the text deals with technical debt and the quality of the codebase, treating them as strategic matters that managers must weigh against product deadlines rather than as chores for individual developers. Other themes include how organisations change over time, how to handle reorganisations and how managers can grow their own careers and those of their staff. The chapters are essays that can be read separately, and each pairs a general principle with examples from experience in software companies. The book is directed at engineering managers, directors and senior engineers who are moving toward leadership and want a structured way of reasoning about teams and organisational design.'),
  ('Accelerate', 'Nicole Forsgren, Jez Humble, Gene Kim', NULL, 'Business', 'IT Revolution', 2018, 2, 2, 'The authors report on several years of research into what separates high-performing technology organisations from the rest. The work is built on large annual surveys of practitioners, analysed with statistical methods, and the book explains both the findings and the way they were obtained so that readers can judge the evidence. Its main claim is that software delivery performance can be measured and that it is linked to wider organisational results such as profitability and market share. Performance is captured by four measures: how quickly changes move from commit to production, how often deployments occur, how long it takes to recover from a failure, and how often changes cause one. The research identifies a set of technical and cultural capabilities that predict these outcomes, including continuous delivery practices, loosely coupled architecture, trunk-based development, monitoring and version control for everything. It also examines leadership, learning culture and the effects of employee satisfaction. A notable conclusion is that speed and stability go together instead of trading off against each other. The final part of the book offers guidance on starting a change programme. It is written for engineering leaders, DevOps practitioners and managers who want data to support decisions about how to improve software delivery.'),
  ('Inspired', 'Marty Cagan', NULL, 'Business', 'Wiley', 2017, 2, 2, 'Cagan describes how strong product companies decide what to build and how their teams are organised to do it. The book is based on the author''s experience in technology firms and argues that many organisations fail because they treat product work as the delivery of features defined by stakeholders. In contrast, it advocates empowered teams made up of product managers, designers and engineers who are given problems to solve rather than lists of tasks. Much of the text concerns product discovery, the process of testing ideas before they are built. Discovery aims to reduce four main risks: whether customers will use the product, whether they can work out how to use it, whether engineers can build it and whether it suits the business. The book covers techniques such as prototyping, user testing and measuring results with small experiments. It also discusses the role of the product manager, the importance of a clear product vision and strategy, and the culture and leadership needed to support this way of working. Case examples contrast traditional project-style development with approaches used by leading technology companies. It is intended for product managers, designers, engineering leaders and executives who want to improve how their organisations create products that people value and that deliver business results.'),

  ('Atomic Habits', 'James Clear', NULL, 'Self-Development', 'Avery', 2018, 4, 4, 'Clear sets out a practical approach to forming good habits and breaking harmful ones, resting on the idea that small daily changes add up through compounding. He argues that results follow from systems rather than goals, and that lasting change is easiest when a habit becomes part of a person''s identity, so that each repetition acts as a vote for the kind of person they want to be. The book breaks behaviour into a loop of cue, craving, response and reward, and builds on it four simple laws: make the behaviour obvious, make it attractive, make it easy and make it satisfying. Each law has a mirror image for stopping a bad habit. Techniques include habit stacking, in which a new action is attached to an existing routine, the two-minute rule for starting small, and designing the physical environment so that good choices need less effort. Clear also discusses the plateau that often comes before visible progress, the role of motivation and how to stay on track after missed days. The writing is clear and example-driven, drawing on stories from sport, business and research in psychology. It is intended for general readers who want straightforward, actionable guidance on improving routines at work, in health and in daily life.'),
  ('Deep Work', 'Cal Newport', NULL, 'Self-Development', 'Grand Central', 2016, 3, 3, 'Newport contrasts two kinds of professional activity: deep work, which means sustained, distraction-free concentration on cognitively demanding tasks, and shallow work, the logistical and administrative tasks that fill most days. He argues that the ability to concentrate is becoming rarer at the same time as it becomes more valuable, because an economy built on complex knowledge rewards people who can learn hard things quickly and produce high-quality results. The first part of the book makes this case, drawing on examples from writers, scientists and business figures. The second part offers rules for building the habit. These include choosing a style of scheduling that fits your life, from long retreats to short daily blocks, creating rituals and boundaries around focused sessions, and treating downtime as part of the process. Newport also recommends training the mind to tolerate boredom, being selective about social media and other tools, and reducing shallow tasks wherever possible. His own routine as an academic and author informs the advice. The book is written for knowledge workers, students, programmers, researchers and writers who feel their attention is being fragmented by email, notifications and open-plan offices, and who want a practical way of protecting time for work that requires real thought.'),
  ('Thinking, Fast and Slow', 'Daniel Kahneman', NULL, 'Self-Development', 'Farrar, Straus and Giroux', 2011, 2, 2, 'Kahneman, a psychologist who won a Nobel Prize in economics, summarises decades of research on how people form judgements and make decisions. The book is organised around two modes of thought. One is fast, automatic and intuitive; the other is slow, effortful and deliberate. He shows how the quick mode usually works well but is prone to systematic errors, and how the slower mode is lazy and often accepts the first answer it is given. Many chapters describe experiments, much of it done with his long-time collaborator Amos Tversky, revealing mental shortcuts such as anchoring, availability and representativeness, and the biases they produce. Other topics include overconfidence, the planning fallacy, the tendency to give too much weight to the story in front of us, and the way the framing of a choice changes what people pick. He explains prospect theory, which describes how people evaluate gains and risks relative to a reference point and feel losses more strongly than equivalent gains. The final sections distinguish the experiencing self from the remembering self and ask what this means for well-being. The book is aimed at general readers, and it is useful to students of economics, psychology, management and policy who want to understand the limits of human reasoning.'),
  ('Mindset', 'Carol S. Dweck', NULL, 'Self-Development', 'Ballantine Books', 2006, 2, 2, 'Dweck, a psychologist at Stanford, presents the results of her research on beliefs about ability and their effect on achievement. She distinguishes two outlooks. People with a fixed mindset assume that intelligence and talent are set traits to be proved, so they avoid challenges and read setbacks as a verdict on themselves. People with a growth mindset believe abilities can be developed through effort, strategy and help from others, so they treat difficulty as part of learning. The book follows these outlooks through several areas of life: school, sport, business, leadership and close relationships. It describes studies of children given different kinds of praise, showing how praising effort and strategy can encourage persistence, while praising innate talent can make students cautious. Dweck also looks at how teachers, coaches and managers shape the mindsets of those they guide, and she warns that the two outlooks are not simple labels; most people hold a mixture that varies by situation. A closing section suggests ways to recognise fixed-mindset thinking and to change it. The book is written for a general audience and is relevant to educators, parents, managers and anyone who wants to understand how attitudes toward failure and effort influence growth.'),
  ('The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People', 'Stephen R. Covey', NULL, 'Self-Development', 'Free Press', 1989, 2, 2, 'Covey offers a framework for personal and professional effectiveness grounded in what he calls the character ethic, the view that lasting success depends on principles such as integrity, fairness and responsibility rather than on techniques of image or influence. The seven habits are arranged in a progression from dependence to independence and then to interdependence. The first three concern self-mastery: be proactive and take responsibility for your choices, begin with the end in mind by clarifying your values and goals, and put first things first by organising time around importance rather than urgency. The next three concern working with others: think in terms of mutual benefit, seek first to understand before being understood, and create synergy through cooperation. The seventh habit is renewal, a regular effort to sustain physical, mental, emotional and spiritual health. The book includes exercises, a well-known time-management matrix and many anecdotes drawn from family life, business and teaching. It has been widely used in corporate training and personal development courses. Readers who prefer scientific evidence may find the approach more philosophical than empirical. It is intended for people at any career stage who want a structured way of thinking about priorities, relationships and daily habits.'),

  -- SLM-16: memoirs that make the semantic search case visible ("losing a parent" finds them by meaning, not by words).
  ('H Is for Hawk', 'Helen Macdonald', NULL, 'Memoir', 'Jonathan Cape', 2014, 2, 2, 'Macdonald is a writer, naturalist and falconer whose father died suddenly, and this book records her grief and mourning in the months that followed. Overwhelmed by bereavement, she did something unusual: she acquired a young goshawk, a notoriously difficult and wild bird of prey, and set about training it. She named the hawk Mabel and spent weeks in the English countryside learning to bond with an animal that is fierce, solitary and unsentimental. The training becomes a way of escaping ordinary life and of facing the emotions that her father''s death had stirred up. Alongside her own story, the book follows the life of the novelist T. H. White, who wrote about his own failed attempt to train a goshawk in the mid-twentieth century, and it uses his troubled biography as a mirror for her own state of mind. Macdonald reflects on nature, solitude, the history of falconry and the ways people project feelings onto animals. The writing is lyrical, precise and honest about the pull of withdrawing from other people. It is suited to readers interested in nature writing, in the experience of mourning, and in how an extraordinary relationship with a wild creature can help a person start to recover.'),
  ('Crying in H Mart', 'Michelle Zauner', NULL, 'Memoir', 'Knopf', 2021, 2, 2, 'Zauner, a musician and writer, recounts the death of her mother from cancer and the grief that followed. She grew up in Oregon as the daughter of a Korean mother and a white American father, and she describes feeling distant from her Korean heritage during adolescence, when she and her mother clashed. When her mother became seriously ill, Zauner returned home to care for her, and the book portrays those months of treatment, hospital stays and difficult conversations with tenderness and candour. After the death, she turned to Korean cooking, shopping and eating as a way to feel connected to her mother, and the book''s title refers to the Asian grocery chain where she sometimes found herself weeping among familiar ingredients. Food is the central language of the memoir; recipes, meals and shared tastes carry the history of the family. The later sections deal with mourning, with building a life and a music career, and with the question of identity for a person of mixed heritage whose link to one culture came mostly through her mother. The tone is intimate, vivid and at times funny. It will interest readers of memoir, those who have experienced bereavement, and anyone curious about food, family and cultural belonging.'),
  ('A Grief Observed', 'C. S. Lewis', NULL, 'Memoir', 'Faber & Faber', 1961, 1, 1, 'Lewis wrote this short memoir after the death of his wife, Joy, and it began as private notebooks in which he set down his thoughts as they came. It was first published under a pseudonym. The text is divided into four brief sections and has no tidy plan; it follows the changing moods of grief, including disbelief, anger, fatigue, physical restlessness and moments of unexpected calm. Lewis, well known for his defence of Christian belief, turns his questioning on his own faith. He asks why God seems absent when he is needed most, tests the comforts he had offered to others, and wonders whether his earlier certainties were stronger than they should have been. He also reflects on memory, on how the image of a dead person can drift from the reality, and on the loneliness of mourning in a world that carries on as usual. The book does not offer a formula for recovery, and its value lies in its honesty and its refusal to hide doubt. Because it is brief, plain in its language and direct in its emotion, it has been read by many people facing bereavement. It is suited to general readers, to those interested in religion and doubt, and to anyone who wants a frank account of how mourning feels from the inside.');

